fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1131", description: some(value: "A ONE-FIELD NAMED TUPLE IS WRITABLE AS A TYPE BUT NOT AS A VALUE — so an operation can declare a parameter no caller can construct an argument for.\n\nMEASURED on the current tree (probes, since removed):\n  * `operation f(t: (a: Int64)) -> Int64 = t.a` — LOADS. The 1-field named tuple TYPE is fine.\n  * `let t = (a: 1)` — PARSE ERROR: \"syntax error near `a: 1`\". So is `g((a: 1))` as a direct argument.\n  * `let t = (a: 1,)` (trailing comma) — PARSE ERROR, but a DIFFERENT and misleading one: \"tuple literal cannot mix positional and named arguments\" plus \"missing `name`\". Nothing positional was written; tree-sitter error-recovered into the 2+ branch with a MISSING node and the converter's all-or-nothing check then fired on the wreckage.\n  * CONTROL: `let t = (a: 1, b: 2)` then `t.a` — Int(1). Two fields work.\n  * The documented single-member distribute-dot `prow.(name)` LOADS, and per kernel-language section 6.8 it 1-COLLAPSES to `prow.name` — a scalar. So it is not a way to build a 1-field tuple either. There is no way to build one.\n\nCAUSE, located: tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js:1404 —\n    tuple_literal: prec(-2, choice(\n      seq('(', ')'),                                                    // unit\n      seq('(', _fn_arg, ',', commaSep1(_fn_arg), optional(','), ')'),   // 2+ elements\n    ))\nwith `paren_expr: seq('(', _term, ')')` beside it, commented \"Grouping only, no tuple. Distinguished from tuple_literal by absence of comma.\" The 2+ requirement is CORRECT for the POSITIONAL case and is why it exists: `(1)` must be grouping, not a 1-tuple, and the comma is what tells them apart. But for a NAMED single element the ambiguity does not exist — `a: 1` is not a `_term`, so `(a: 1)` can never be a parenthesized expression. The restriction is over-broad for the named case, and that over-breadth is what produces an unconstructible type.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH FIXING RATHER THAN DOCUMENTING AWAY (though see the alternative below). It is not a curiosity: a single-entry name-keyed map is exactly the shape a caller needs whenever an API takes a record of column-to-something, and it is the case those APIs are used for most often. It is what killed one of WI-731's candidate designs for the relation `rename` operator — a map argument written as a tuple literal has no spelling at arity one, which is precisely the single-column rename the ticket exists for.\n\nFIRST TASK — DECIDE, DO NOT ASSUME. Probe whether adding a named-only single-element alternative to `tuple_literal` is conflict-free, the way proposal 056 probed the `...` marker before adopting it. The likely rival is a parenthesized TYPED BINDER in expression position (`lambda (a: Int64) -> ...`), so the probe must cover lambda parameters, and the full corpus must be re-measured, not just the new case. If it is conflict-free, admit it. If it is NOT, then the restriction is a real language rule and must be WRITTEN DOWN — kernel-language section 4.5 states the named-tuple value form and says nothing about a minimum arity — together with a diagnostic that names the actual rule at the actual span, and a note on the type side that such a parameter cannot be given a literal argument.\n\nEITHER WAY, THE TRAILING-COMMA DIAGNOSTIC IS A SEPARATE DEFECT AND MUST GO: \"tuple literal cannot mix positional and named arguments\" is a false statement about a program that mixed nothing. It comes from parse/convert.rs:2043 running on an error-recovered node. Whatever is decided above, `(a: 1,)` must report the real fault at the real span.\n\nACCEPTANCE: either `let t = (a: 1)` parses and `t.a` DRIVES to 1 and a 1-field tuple is passable to a parameter declared `(a: Int64)` — with `(1)` asserted to STAY grouping as the control, since that is the ambiguity the arity rule exists for, and the tree-sitter corpus extended and fully re-measured — OR the refusal is deliberate, recorded in kernel-language section 4.5 with its reason, and reported by a diagnostic that names the rule rather than a fault the author did not commit; in both outcomes `(a: 1,)` no longer claims a positional/named mix; cargo-test green via scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none), ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "claude", at: "2026-08-18T14:56:22Z"))

fact Feedback(workitem: "WI-1131", author: "claude", content: "DECIDED: the named-only arity-one alternative IS conflict-free, so it was admitted rather than written down as a rule. `tree-sitter generate` reports no conflict for it. The rival the ticket named — a parenthesized TYPED BINDER in expression position — never competes: a lambda's parameter is a `_pattern`, so `lambda (a: Int64) -> a` reaches `pattern_typed`, and the two existing corpus rows for it (`expressions.txt`, `lambda (y: Int) -> …` and `lambda (acc: Int, elem: Int) -> …`) stayed green. Corpus re-measured in full: 205 -> 209, all green.\n\nGRAMMAR (`tuple_literal`), four arms now:\n  * `()` and the 2+ form keep `prec(-2)`, but PER-ALTERNATIVE, not on the `choice`. That hoist is load-bearing and was the one real trap: at -2 against the default 0 the wrapper also out-ranked the `named_arg`/`_positional_fn_arg` -> `_fn_arg` reduction the 2+ arm needs, so the one-element arms were silently resolved away and `(a: 1,)` stayed an error-recovered wreck even after the arm was added. With the actions equal it becomes a real choice, declared as the GLR conflict `[$.tuple_literal, $._fn_arg]` and decided by what follows the comma.\n  * `('(', named_arg, optional(','), ')')` — the capability.\n  * `('(', _positional_fn_arg, ',', ')')` — parses a form the language does not have, ONLY so `convert.rs` can refuse it at the literal's own span (`TUPLE_ARITY_ONE_IS_NAMED_ONLY`): the WI-763 parse-permissive / convert-strict style `tuple_type` already uses for a lone positional `(A)`. `_positional_fn_arg` is `_fn_arg` minus `named_arg`, factored so the two cannot drift — the first cut spelled it `$._term`, which is narrower by two forms, and `(?x: T,)` / `(lambda y -> y,)` fell straight back into the `missing `name`` wreck.\n\nMEASURED (rustland): `let t = (a: 1)` then `t.a` -> 1; `f((a: 1))` on `f(t: (a: Int64)) -> Int64 = t.a` -> 1; `mk() -> (a: Int64) = (a: 1)` then `mk().a` -> 1 (the op-return channel, since §4.5 now claims inhabitation \"in any position\"); `(a: 1,)` -> 1. CONTROL: `(1)` stays `paren_expr`, evaluates to the scalar 1, and does NOT conform to `(a: Int64)`. `(1,)`, `(?x: T,)`, `(lambda y -> y,)` are each refused by the arity-one rule, with `!contains` controls pinning that neither the mix claim nor `missing `name`` survives.\n\nBACK-OUT (`tuple_literal` restored to its two-arm form, the conflict declaration and the converter guard with it): 6 red, 3 green, and the pre-fix diagnostics reproduce verbatim — `syntax error near `a: 1``, and the `cannot mix positional and named arguments` + `missing `name`` pair on `(a: 1,)`. GREEN EITHER WAY by design and named as such: `(1)`-stays-grouping, the 2-component tuple, and `(1, b: 2)`-still-reports-the-mix.\n\nTHE TRAILING-COMMA DEFECT IS GONE, both halves. `(a: 1,)` now parses with NO diagnostics at all. `(1,)` is still refused — arity one is named-only — but by a message stating that rule at the right span.\n\nDIVERGENCE THE SHARED WI-777 PARITY CORPUS SURFACED, three ways. Scaland already BUILT `(a: 1)` and nothing measured it; it REJECTED every trailing comma inside a LITERAL, `(1, 2,)` included, which rustland has always accepted (a `~/` cut inside the element repetition committed to an element that was not there); and it accepted `(1,)` silently as grouping. All three fixed (`!\")\"` before the cut; the same refusal, spelled to match rustland's wording). The corpus's only trailing-comma case was a tuple TYPE, which is why nothing saw the literal divergence — four cases added, and each case in `wi777_parser_parity_corpus_test`'s control note is now named by the MEASURED back-out that reddens it, and on which side.\n\nSPEC: §4.5's `TupleLiteral` grammar gains the one-element named form (and the 2+ trailing comma it always accepted); \"Disambiguation (term position)\" rewritten, since its old \"`(a)` with no comma is grouping … No lookahead needed\" was contradicted by both the new arm and the declared conflict; the closing paragraph flipped from \"no arity-matching literal\" to the two inhabitation routes. §6.8's 1-collapse justification carried the same retired claim and was corrected — the collapse itself is unchanged and still deliberate (WI-776). Two more sites carried it and were refreshed: `typing.rs`'s `one_collapse_note` (its advice now names the one-field literal, not only \"supply a wider tuple\") and `wi766_one_component_tuple_type_test`'s module note, which still taught that `(a: 1)` is a syntax error.\n\nSTRONGEST CONTROL: full parse-tree diff, pre-change grammar vs new, over all 1297 `.anthill` files in the repo. The ONLY structural differences are the three new WI-1131 parity fixtures. No silent shape change anywhere.\n\nTESTS: `scripts/test.sh` full workspace — 35 result lines, 29 binaries, 5163 passed, 0 failed. `sbt test` — 522 passed, 0 failed. `npx tree-sitter test` — 209/209.\n\n`/code-review high` was run; all four of its findings are fixed above (the `$._term`-too-narrow arm, the stale wi766 module note, the contradicted §4.5 disambiguation summary, and the missing shared-corpus case for a 2+ literal trailing comma).", at: "2026-08-18T14:56:26Z")
